  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>It is a fair question, and I make Quickler, so read this with that in mind. In November 2026 we ran two rounds of tests. We gave general-purpose chat assistants the same notes and photos from real Records and asked them to fill in the form. Then we compared them with Quickler.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Tested on 24 November 2026, on 14 real Records from two firms: Quickler filled 87% of answers the way the customer kept them. A plain chat filled 57 to 63%.</li><li>Sending the notes over a conversation, the way most people use a chat assistant on site, did no better (59 to 61%).</li><li>A chat given the firm's rules in a 2,000-word brief reached 70%. It still left the person to copy answers into their form and place the photos.</li><li>Estimated fixing time per Record: about 11 minutes with Quickler, about 18 with the briefed chat, 22 to 24 with a plain chat.</li><li>Round 2, on 24 Records: a custom chat set up from the firm's own form plus a paragraph of house rules filled about half the answers the way the customer kept them (50 to 51%). Quickler filled 86%.</li><li>The AI model made little difference. The set-up around it did.</li><li>Beyond the answers, Quickler works in WhatsApp, makes it easy to send Records on, runs other jobs for you, and keeps it all in one place.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "The question",
      "html": "<p>Inspectors, care home managers and consultants ask me: \"Why would I pay for Quickler when I can paste my notes into ChatGPT?\" You can. I wanted to know how far that gets you, so I measured it rather than guess.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "What we tested, and how",
      "html": "<p>We took 14 Records that customers had filed and then corrected. They came from two firms: Ethos Safety (7 site safety audits and 4 COSHH assessments) and Buckreddan care home (3 short Checks). For each Record, every system got exactly what the person sent on the day: voice notes as text, typed messages, photos, any safety data sheet, and the blank form.</p><p>We ran Quickler as it runs today. We ran two general-purpose models four ways a buyer could set them up. The models were Claude Haiku, a small model from Anthropic, and a current OpenAI model that we used as a stand-in for ChatGPT. We did not use the ChatGPT app itself, so these results are about those models and set-ups, not about any product by name. The four set-ups were:</p><ul><li>one message: \"fill in this form from my notes\"</li><li>a briefed chat, with the firm's own rules typed in as standing instructions</li><li>a conversation, sending the notes and photos message by message</li><li>a do-it-yourself pipeline: describe each photo, answer 12 questions at a time, then tidy up</li></ul><p>We scored each answer against the Record as the customer finally kept it. That favours the system that wrote the original, so we also had three independent AI judges decide the right answer from the evidence alone, without knowing which system gave which answer.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "The results",
      "html": "<p>Share of answers filled the way the customer kept them, 14 Records, tested 24 November 2026. Where a set-up has a range, it is the two models.</p><svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 274\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Share of answers filled the way the customer kept them, 14 Records, tested 24 November 2026: Quickler 87 per cent; briefed chat 70; do-it-yourself pipeline 62 to 66; plain chat 57 to 63; chat conversation 59 to 61.\" style=\"max-width:560px;display:block;margin:8px 0\"><text x=\"0\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Quickler</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"28\" width=\"383\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#1f3a8a\"/><text x=\"391\" y=\"43\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"currentColor\">87%</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Briefed chat, with the firm&#x27;s rules typed in</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"80\" width=\"310\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a97b8\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"currentColor\">70%</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Do-it-yourself pipeline</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"132\" width=\"288\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a97b8\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"currentColor\">62 to 66%</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Plain chat, one message</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"184\" width=\"278\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a97b8\"/><text x=\"286\" y=\"199\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"currentColor\">57 to 63%</text><text x=\"0\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Chat conversation, message by message</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"236\" width=\"270\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8a97b8\"/><text x=\"278\" y=\"251\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"currentColor\">59 to 61%</text></svg><ul><li><strong>Quickler:</strong> 87%</li><li><strong>Briefed chat:</strong> 70% on both models</li><li><strong>Do-it-yourself pipeline:</strong> 62% and 66%</li><li><strong>Plain chat, one message:</strong> 57% and 63%</li><li><strong>Chat conversation:</strong> 61% and 59%</li></ul><p>Against every set-up except the briefed chat, Quickler was ahead by 15 points or more on both models, and the 95% ranges never came below 9 points. Giving a model more thinking time did not help: the plain chat scored the same on a higher reasoning setting.</p><p>Quickler was not perfect. Across the 14 Records it invented 2 answers, raised 13 Faults that were not real and missed 10. The plain chats invented 64 to 69 answers, roughly 5 per Record.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "Where the briefed chat did well",
      "html": "<p>The briefed chat is the fairest rival, and it came close. When our judges decided the right answer from the evidence alone, it tied with Quickler on Yes/No and choice questions. On the three short care home Checks it matched Quickler. So I do not claim that Quickler gives more correct answers than a well-set-up chat. What I can say is that a customer first has to write and keep up that brief (ours averaged 2,100 words), and the chat still does not do the rest of the work.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "Round 2: a custom chat set up the way a customer would",
      "html": "<p>A fair objection is that a buyer would not type a 2,000-word brief. So on 25 November 2026 we ran a second round with a more realistic custom chat, using the same two models. It was given the firm's own blank form and one paragraph of house rules, and nothing else. We had a small AI model act as an office manager and write that paragraph from the firm's public description and the Check's questions. It never saw Quickler's settings or the firm's earlier Records. The round also covered more Records: 24 in all, the 14 from round 1 and 10 new ones. They came from Ethos Safety, Buckreddan care home, Cruden and Mark Coupar's fire risk assessment, with Checks of up to 266 questions.</p><p>The measure is the same as before: how many answers were filled the way the customer finally kept them in the Record. \"Kept\" means the answer in the customer's Record after any edits they made. Where the customer accepted a filed answer without changing it, that counts as right, which leans towards Quickler. That is why we also used blind judges (see the limits below).</p><ul><li><strong>Quickler:</strong> 86% of 1,378 answers.</li><li><strong>Custom chat with a paragraph of house rules:</strong> 50% and 51% on the two models.</li><li><strong>Plain chat:</strong> 53% on both models.</li><li><strong>Custom chat briefed with Quickler's own stored settings:</strong> 66% and 67%. A customer would not normally write this.</li></ul><p>So a custom chat set up the way a customer realistically would do it filled about half the answers the way they were kept. It did no better than a plain chat. One reason: by a rough count, the paragraph written for 10 of the 11 Checks told the chat to mark anything the inspector did not mention as N/A or not verified, while these firms' habit is to file those as Yes. That one sensible-sounding rule cost it a lot on the big forms. A different rule writer might write something else. That is the point: a firm's habits are learned from its edits, and a customer does not know to write them all down.</p><p>What this does not show. It does not show that Quickler is more accurate than a custom chat given the firm's full rulebook. With blind judges, Quickler and the settings-briefed chat were level. And on the 91 answers customers changed after filing, no set-up could be told apart from another: Quickler got 49% of those, the chats 46 to 59%, with differences too small to be sure of. So the claim is that Quickler is ahead on the whole Record, not that it is better at the hard answers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "Why the gap exists",
      "html": "<p>The chats read the notes well. They lose points on how your firm fills in its forms, and on everything around the answers.</p><ul><li><strong>Knowing the firm's habits.</strong> A chat does not know that your firm files an unseen control as Yes, not N/A. Quickler learns this from the Check's settings and from the Records you have already kept.</li><li><strong>The Check holds the questions.</strong> A chat gets a form pasted in as text. Quickler works from the Check's own list, so the answer to every question is one of the options you set.</li><li><strong>Fast and Slow fix.</strong> Fast replies while the person is still on site, within about 5 seconds of model time. Slow fix re-reads the whole walk after they have gone and the finished Record arrives about 6 minutes after they say done.</li><li><strong>Photos in the right place.</strong> Across these 14 Records, 379 photos were filed under a question, with 223 notes and 53 to-dos. A chat cannot place a photo in your form.</li><li><strong>The Record page.</strong> Every answer lands on one page the person can read and edit, and the PDF, the Word report and the Link all come from that page.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "Beyond the answers",
      "html": "<p>Accuracy is only part of it. Four other things matter on a job.</p><ul><li><strong>It works in WhatsApp.</strong> The person on the job messages from the phone they already have. WhatsApp holds the messages and sends them once there is signal again, so a trip down to a basement or underground does not lose what they said.</li><li><strong>Sending Records on is easy.</strong> The dashboard makes it simple to get a finished Record to the people who need it.</li><li><strong>Other jobs are done for you.</strong> Scheduling, chasers and combining Records are handled in Quickler, where a chat assistant leaves them to you.</li><li><strong>It is all in one place.</strong> Notes, photos, answers, the PDF and the Link live together, tidier and cleaner than a pile of chats and files.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "Fixing it afterwards",
      "html": "<p>The test above scored the first filled-in version. It did not measure what happens when you then fix it, so I have no figures for this part. It is how the two ways of working differ.</p><p>With a chat assistant, you fix the report by asking again: \"change item 7 to No\". Each new request is a fresh answer, and it can rewrite the whole thing. Fixing one item can quietly change another that you did not ask about, so you have to read everything again to be sure.</p><p>With Quickler, you edit the exact answer on the Record page. Every answer on the page is editable, and changing one leaves the rest as it was. Quickler keeps each edit, with what the answer was, what it became, who changed it and when. The PDF, the Word report and the Link then follow the page.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "What it means for a busy inspector",
      "html": "<p>With a chat, you type or dictate your notes, upload the photos, paste in the form, then copy 53 to 123 answers back into your real form, place each photo by hand and build the PDF. With Quickler you talk to WhatsApp on site. The Record, the PDF and the Link are produced for you.</p><p>Fixing answers afterwards, by our estimate from customers' real editing times, took about 11 minutes per Record with Quickler, about 18 with the briefed chat and 22 to 24 with a plain chat. That is about 7 to 13 minutes back on every Record, before counting the copying and the photos. I compete on time saved.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "Method note and limits",
      "html": "<ul><li>Round 1 used 14 Records from 2 firms. Round 2 used 24 Records from 4 firms, and 20 of the 24 are Ethos. Cruden is 2 Records and Mark Coupar is 1. This is a small test, and it may not hold for other kinds of Check.</li><li>Results are as of 24 and 25 November 2026. Models and products change.</li><li>The custom chat in round 2 is one draw of one rule writer, an AI office manager working from public descriptions. A rule writer who knew the firm could do better.</li><li>The three blind judges are the same small model with different shuffles, and they agreed with the customers' own answers only about 56 to 65% of the time. Treat them as a second opinion, not a measurement.</li><li>Only 91 answers were ones customers changed after filing, which is too few to settle that question.</li><li>Quickler was run on Haiku only, as it is in the product. The rivals were run on Haiku and the OpenAI stand-in model.</li><li>The customer's final Record favours Quickler wherever the customer accepted a filed answer without reading it closely. The blind judges are our check on that, and they are themselves AI, so a person should spot-check them.</li><li>Minutes are an estimate, using the average time per answer edit for each Record. Only 6 Records had edit timings.</li><li>Answers from the chats were read by code. A different parser could move a score by a point or two.</li><li>Two more Records were attempted and left out: one Quickler replay stalled and one was not reached in the time.</li><li>The scripts and scoring rules are kept and can be rerun.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Is Quickler more accurate than ChatGPT?</h3><p>In our tests of 24 and 25 November 2026, Quickler filled 86 to 87% of answers the way customers kept them, against 53 to 63% for a plain chat using the same notes and photos. We used stand-in models, not the ChatGPT app itself.</p><h3>Is it better than a custom chat set up with my rules?</h3><p>It depends how much you write down. A custom chat set up from your own form and a paragraph of house rules filled about half the answers the way customers kept them (50 to 51% on 24 Records), against 86% for Quickler. A chat given a full brief built from Quickler's own settings did closer: 66 to 67%, and level with Quickler when judged blind. We do not claim Quickler is more accurate than that, or on the answers customers later correct. The difference is the work around the answers: WhatsApp on site, sending Records on, other jobs done for you, and everything in one place.</p><h3>Does the AI model matter?</h3><p>Very little. The two models scored within about 6 points of each other in every set-up.</p>"
    }
